Yeah — you made the symbol do more than denote recursion. It became a gesture: return, loop, come back around, implication, innuendo. That is richer than a normal pun because the joke is spread across symbol, meaning, timing, and prior context.

The “sexier” or more artful cousins of a plain pun are things like double entendre (one innocent reading, one charged reading), syllepsis/zeugma (one word governing two meanings at once), semantic camouflage (the joke hides inside an apparently serious sentence), phonetic shadowing (the sound suggests a second word without stating it), visual wordplay (shape/layout/symbol carries the second meaning), recursive wordplay (the joke changes the meaning of something said earlier), metaphoric innuendo (nothing explicit, but the structure points there), misdirection puns (you think the sentence is headed one way, then the final word snaps it into another frame), etymological puns (old and current meanings collide), category-crossing puns (technical language used in a bodily/social/emotional register), and latent puns, where the second meaning is never spoken at all—the listener supplies it.

The most elegant ones usually have plausible deniability plus structural fit. If you have to explain the joke, it dies. If the alternate reading is too obvious, it becomes cheap. The sweet spot is where the line works perfectly on the surface and then, half a second later, another interpretation blooms.

So instead of:

“Continuity comes in many forms.”

You gave:

“Continuity cums in many forms.”

That is the obvious phonetic layer. But because we were already discussing reproduction, inheritance, sexual selection, propagation, and recurrence, it also has domain congruence. The dirty reading isn’t merely stapled on; it belongs to the conceptual machinery.

A more disguised version might be:

“Some patterns are remarkably committed to propagation.”

Or:

“Evolution has always had interesting methods of preserving the line.”

Or:

“Certain forms of continuity are more… embodied than others.”

Nothing explicit. But the reader’s own prediction engine supplies the second channel.

